.Dd November 11, 2026
.Dt STAMPD 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm stampd
.Nd STAMP Session-Reflector
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl \&Ad
.Op Fl C Ar source Ns Op : Ns Ar error
.Op Fl D Ar dscps
.Op Fl f Ar file
.Op Fl K Ar keyfile
.Op Fl m Cm stateless | stateful
.Op Fl P Ar pidfile
.Op Fl p Ar port
.Op Fl R Ar rate
.Op Fl r Ar rate Ns Op / Ns Ar burst
.Op Fl u Ar user
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a Session-Reflector of the Simple Two-way Active Measurement
Protocol (STAMP, RFC 8762), unauthenticated and authenticated, with
the Session-Sender Identifier of RFC 8972; it also answers a TWAMP Light Session-Sender
(RFC 5357 Appendix I) that pads its packets to 44 octets.
It listens on UDP
.Ar port
(862) of every address, IPv4 and IPv6, in the FIB it is started in
.Pq Xr setfib 1 :
one
.Nm
a FIB, as its
.Xr rc 8
script runs them.
.Pp
The time a packet came in is the kernel's
.Pq Dv SO_TIMESTAMP No with Dv SO_TS_REALTIME ;
the time its reply leaves,
.Xr clock_gettime 2 Ns 's
just before it is sent; both in NTP's format, the error estimate from
.Xr ntp_gettime 2 .
The TTL or hop limit the packet came with is reported, and the reply
leaves from the address the packet came to, with TTL 255.
.Pp
It is no amplifier: a packet shorter than 44 octets, RFC 8762's base,
is not answered, and a reply is exactly as long as its packet, what
follows the base (padding, TLVs) copied.
A sender's Timestamp Information TLV (RFC 8972 section 4.3) is filled
in: the clock's synchronisation source
.Pf ( Cm ntp
when
.Xr ntp_gettime 2
says the clock is synchronised,
.Cm local
.Pq free-running
when not, or as
.Fl C
says) and how the times were taken
.Pf ( Cm hw
when the kernel says the hardware took the time a packet came in, else
.Cm sw ;
.Cm sw
the time its reply leaves).
So is a Class of Service TLV (section 4.4): the DSCP and ECN the
request came with
.Pq Dv IP_RECVTOS , IPV6_RECVTCLASS
in DSCP2 and ECN, and the reply sent with the DSCP the sender asked
for, DSCP1
.Pq Dv IP_TOS , IPV6_TCLASS ,
unless
.Fl D
refuses it: the reply then carries the DSCP the request came with,
the TLV's RP 1.
A Direct Measurement TLV (section 4.5) is filled with the requests
the session received (one over the rate limits among them) and the
replies it sent; stateless, with zeros, there being no session to
count in.
A Location TLV (section 4.2) is filled with the ports the request came
to and from, and the addresses its sub-TLVs ask for, in the request's
family: the source as the request came, the destination the address it
came to; a source MAC, which no UDP socket sees, as an EUI-64 of zero;
none of them with
.Cm location hide .
Another TLV but Extra Padding is marked unrecognised (its U flag); a
Timestamp Information TLV of under 4 octets, a Class of Service TLV not
of 4, a Direct Measurement TLV not of 12 or a Location TLV of under 4
or whose sub-TLVs run past it malformed (M).
What follows the base is taken as TLVs only when it is TLVs to its end,
none of type 0; anything else, padding, is copied as it is.
Nor is a packet from port 0 or from
.Ar port
answered, nor one over the rate limits; those are dropped silently,
their count logged when it has grown, every 5 seconds at most.
.Pp
In the authenticated mode (RFC 8762 section 4.4) a request is 112
octets or more, its fields apart and an HMAC of its first 96 octets,
HMAC-SHA-256 truncated to 128 bits, and RFC 8972's HMAC TLV after its
other TLVs.
It is answered, under the same key, only when its HMAC verifies under
a key of the key file for its SSID, or for any SSID, and what follows
its base is well-formed TLVs to its end; otherwise it is dropped,
never answered, and the count of those is logged as the rate's is.
A request whose HMAC TLV is wrong or missing is answered with its TLVs
as they came, each flagged I (its integrity failed), none filled.
A request not authenticated is answered as before, unless
.Fl A .
A request whose HMAC verified is limited by rate buckets apart from
the others', its source's and a total of its own as large as
.Fl R ,
so that a flood of requests not authenticated, whose source addresses
anybody can write, does not make the authenticated sessions look
lossy; nor does such a request ever replace or count in an
authenticated session.
.Pp
Bound,
.Nm
writes its pidfile and, run by root, gives up root: it changes its
root to
.Pa /var/empty
and runs as
.Ar user
(and its group).
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width "-r rate[/burst]"
.It Fl A
Answer only requests of the authenticated mode.
.It Fl C Ar source Ns Op : Ns Ar error
The clock as it is, for one that
.Xr ntp_gettime 2
does not know is synchronised: by PTP without the kernel's PLL, by a
GNSS receiver, or on the same host as the sender's, whose clock it is.
.Ar source
is
.Cm ntp , ptp , ssu , gnss
or
.Cm local
(free-running: not synchronised), and
.Ar error
the clock's error in seconds (default the least the Error Estimate
says).
The replies' Error Estimate then says synchronised with that error, as
the Timestamp Information TLV says that source.
.It Fl D Ar dscps
The DSCPs a Class of Service TLV may not ask a reply to carry:
.Cm all ,
.Cm none
(the default), or a list of them, 0 to 63, by commas.
.It Fl d
Stay in the foreground and log to the standard error too, debugging
messages among them.
.It Fl f Ar file
The configuration file; it must be there.
Without
.Fl f ,
.Pa /usr/local/etc/stampd.conf
is read when it is there.
.It Fl K Ar keyfile
The key file; it must be there.
Without
.Fl K ,
.Pa /usr/local/etc/stampd.keys
is read when it is there.
It is read before root is given up and kept open: see
.Sx KEYS .
.It Fl m Cm stateless | stateful
.Cm stateless
copies the sender's sequence number into the reply;
.Cm stateful ,
the default, counts the packets reflected in each session (RFC 8762
section 4.3.2): a session the FIB, the sender's address and port, the
address the packet came to, and the SSID, so that the sender tells
loss on the way out from loss on the way back.
A session ends after 300 seconds without a packet; 4096 are kept at
most.
A session made by an authenticated request is apart from one made by an
unauthenticated request of the same key.
When they are all kept, the oldest made unauthenticated is replaced,
an authenticated one only by an authenticated request and when there
is none made unauthenticated; a request not authenticated is then
dropped, and the count of those logged as the rate's is.
.It Fl P Ar pidfile
Where to write the process ID.
.It Fl p Ar port
The UDP port; default 862.
.It Fl R Ar rate
Replies a second to all sources together, its burst as many; default
2000, 0 for no limit.
Authenticated requests have a total of their own, as large.
.It Fl r Ar rate Ns Op / Ns Ar burst
Replies a second to one source, a token bucket each, and the burst it
allows; default 50/100, the burst twice the rate when not given, 0 for
no limit.
A source is an IPv4 address (an IPv4-mapped IPv6 address alike) or an
IPv6 /64, one host's or one link's: the senders of a /64 share its
bucket.
Authenticated requests have buckets apart.
The buckets are a table of 4096 keyed by a secret hash, a new source
taking the oldest of its set, so that no sender chooses whom it
displaces.
.It Fl u Ar user
The user it runs as once bound, when started by root; default
.Ql _stampd .
.El
.Sh CONFIGURATION
The configuration file has a setting a line, a word and its value,
.Ql #
starting a comment:
.Bl -tag -width "rate-total rate"
.It Cm mode Cm stateless | stateful
As
.Fl m .
.It Cm rate Ar rate Ns Op / Ns Ar burst
As
.Fl r .
.It Cm rate-total Ar rate
As
.Fl R .
.It Cm dscp-refuse Ar dscps
As
.Fl D .
.It Cm location Cm show | hide
Whether a Location TLV says the ports and addresses a request came
with; default
.Cm show .
.It Cm auth Cm required | optional
.Cm required
as
.Fl A ;
default
.Cm optional .
.El
.Pp
What the command line sets is kept over the file's.
.Sh KEYS
The key file has a key a line,
.Dq Ar ssid key :
the SSID of the sessions it is for, 0 to 65535, or
.Ql *
for any, and the key, 32 to 128 hexadecimal digits (16 to 64 octets);
.Ql #
starts a comment.
A request's key is the first of its SSID's, or of any SSID's, under
which its HMAC verifies.
The file must be a regular file its owner alone may read and write
(mode 0600 at most), owned by root (or by the user that runs
.Nm
unprivileged); otherwise
.Nm
does not start.
No key is ever logged; an error says the line's number.
.Pp
It is opened and read before root is given up, and kept open:
.Dv SIGHUP
reads it again through that descriptor, so it is to be rewritten in
place (truncated and written, as the shell's
.Ql >
does), not replaced by another file, which a reload cannot open once
root is given up.
A file replaced, or one that cannot be read or has an error, is logged
and the keys kept as they were; a restart reads it.
A key file made while
.Nm
runs without one is read when it is started again.
.Sh SIGNALS
.Bl -tag -width SIGTERM
.It Dv SIGHUP
Read the configuration file and the key file again; its sessions are
kept unless it becomes stateless.
A file that cannot be read, or has an error, is logged and the
configuration kept.
.It Dv SIGINFO , SIGUSR1
Log the reflector's counters, a line at
.Dv LOG_INFO :
the requests answered, those dropped by reason
.Pf ( Cm short :
under 44 octets;
.Cm port :
from port 0 or
.Ar port ;
.Cm rate :
over a rate limit;
.Cm nomem :
too long to answer;
.Cm auth :
failing authentication;
.Cm unauth :
not authenticated, with
.Fl A ;
.Cm full :
not authenticated, the sessions all authenticated), those answered
whose HMAC TLV was wrong
.Pq Cm tlv_failed
and the sessions kept, as in
.Bd -literal -offset indent
answered=120 dropped: short=0 port=0 rate=3 nomem=0 auth=1 unauth=0
full=0 tlv_failed=0 sessions=2
.Ed
.Pp
(one line).
.It Dv SIGTERM , SIGINT
Log the drops and stop.
.El
.Sh RC.CONF VARIABLES
The
.Xr rc 8
script runs one
.Nm
for each FIB of
.Va stampd_fibs ,
each by
.Xr rc.subr 8 Ns 's
.Va stampd_fib
.Pq Xr setfib 1 ,
its pidfile
.Pa /var/run/stampd/fib Ns Ar N Ns Pa .pid .
.Bl -tag -width stampd_rundir
.It Va stampd_enable
.Dq YES
to run it.
.It Va stampd_fibs
The FIBs, by number, separated by spaces or commas; default
.Dq 0 .
.It Va stampd_flags
The flags of each.
.It Va stampd_rundir
Where the pidfiles are; default
.Pa /var/run/stampd .
.El
.Pp
.Ql service stampd reload
starts those of FIBs listed that do not run, stops those of FIBs no
longer listed, and sends the others
.Dv SIGHUP .
.Ss One socket for every FIB
With
.Va net.inet.udp.bind_all_fibs
1 (a loader tunable), a socket of FIB 0 hears the packets of every
FIB, its replies routed by FIB 0's table, and a socket of another FIB
cannot bind the port: the script then runs FIB 0's alone, and says so.
Set it to 0 in
.Xr loader.conf 5
for each FIB's own.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /usr/local/etc/stampd.conf -compact
.It Pa /usr/local/etc/stampd.conf
The configuration file.
.It Pa /usr/local/etc/stampd.keys
The key file.
.It Pa /var/run/stampd/
The pidfiles of the instances the
.Xr rc 8
script runs.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh EXAMPLES
A reflector in FIBs 0 and 1, by
.Xr rc.conf 5 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sysrc stampd_enable=YES stampd_fibs="0 1"
service stampd start
.Ed
.Pp
One by hand, in FIB 2, stateless, in the foreground:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
setfib 2 stampd -d -m stateless
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr setfib 1 ,
.Xr stamp 1 ,
.Xr libstamp 3 ,
.Xr rc.conf 5 ,
.Xr rc.subr 8
